{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1510,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a few hours, this sloppy wet concrete will harden into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4600,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a solid, which, after a few days will be strong\nenough to hold many tons of weight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9670,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pasta also starts in a moist amorphous state,\nhardens after a few hours, and it can also hold"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14830,
    "dDurationMs": 750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of weight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15580,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s an important difference between\ncapellini and concrete – noodles dry out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20220,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(which you can tell because when you add water,\nthey re-absorb it and become soft again),"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24050,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while concrete sets (you can pour as much\nwater on it as you want, and it won’t become"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28490,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soggy - which is good because you don’t\nwant buildings and bridges to get soggy when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31970,
    "dDurationMs": 789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it rains)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32759,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The distinction is that when pasta is made,\nwater simply evaporates out of the dough,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37109,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaving behind a desiccated, starchy scaffolding\nwith plenty of holes \u0026 gaps ready to re-absorb"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42010,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water in the future and re-soften the dough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44600,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When concrete sets, on the other hand, calcium\noxide, silicon dioxide and other molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49510,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the cement chemically combine with water\nmolecules to create new compounds like calcium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53859,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silicate hydrate. These molecules grow into\ncrystalline fibers that bond with each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58639,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and literally cement together rock, sand,\nmetal or whatever else you put into the mix."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63579,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when concrete hardens, it’s not drying\nout, because water isn’t leaving it – water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68120,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is reacting with and becoming PART of the\ncement. In fact, concrete continues to set"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72750,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for months and even years after it’s poured,\nincreasing in strength over time and enabling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77300,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the construction of miraculously strong and\ndurable structures, like the 2000 foot tall CN Tower in Toronto"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83240,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the 2000 year old Pantheon in Rome."
    } ]
  } ]
}
